  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3y+2?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,2),(3,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=-3(2)+2=-4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,2)}=-3(2)+2=-4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-1)}=-3(-1)+2=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/e98bd7245cbbd5854bd461a8fa1bbb42cef768ee842e71cc3372fa7fed85d804.png)",
